course: biology
lesson: 281
title: Extinct forever: the dodo, the passenger pigeon, the thylacine and more
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Extinct means gone for ever: not one of that kind is left anywhere, and no zoo, no film and no wish can bring it back as it was.
explain: The dodo is the famous example: cartoons show it as a silly bird, but it was a big flightless pigeon on the island of Mauritius that had never met a predator, so it had no reason to fear or fly.
explain: Then sailors arrived in the 1600s with rats, pigs and monkeys that ate dodos and their eggs on the ground, and by about 1690 the dodo was gone.
explain: Most kinds of living things that ever lived are extinct, so dying out is natural, but today species are vanishing much faster than normal because of people, and that speed is what worries scientists.
fact: The dodo was a big flightless pigeon from Mauritius, with small wings it could not fly with, that lived with no predators; sailors, rats, pigs and monkeys arrived in the 1600s, and it was gone by about 1690.
fact: Dodos were not stupid: they had never met predators, and animals on islands often lose their fear and their flight; the real problem was the animals people brought.
fact: In the 1800s billions of passenger pigeons darkened the skies of North America; mass hunting and forest cutting killed them all, and the last one, Martha, died in Cincinnati Zoo in 1914.
fact: The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped, meat-eating marsupial, an animal whose babies grow in a pouch, shaped like a wolf; farmers hunted it for rewards, and the last known one died in Hobart Zoo in 1936.
fact: People still report thylacine sightings, but none have been proven.
fact: Steller's sea cow was a giant, gentle cousin of the manatee, up to about 8 metres long; Europeans found it near Russia's islands in 1741 and hunted it to extinction in 27 years.
fact: The great auk was a bird of the North Atlantic with small wings it could not fly with and the first bird to be called a penguin; the last known pair was killed in Iceland in 1844, and sailors later gave the name penguin to the birds in the south.
fact: The quagga was a zebra with stripes only on its front half; it was hunted out in South Africa, and the last died in a zoo in 1883.
fact: DNA showed the quagga was a kind of plains zebra, and a project has bred zebras that look like quaggas again.
fact: The golden toad of Costa Rica was last seen in 1989; a deadly fungus disease and a changing climate are blamed, and this fungus threatens many frogs worldwide.
fact: The baiji, or Yangtze river dolphin, lived only in China's Yangtze River; fishing nets, boats and dams caused its loss, and it was declared probably extinct after a search in 2006 found none.
fact: More than 99 percent of all species that ever lived are extinct, but today they vanish much faster than normal because of people.
fact: Animals thought extinct that turn up alive again are called Lazarus species, like the coelacanth fish, the takahe bird of New Zealand and the Lord Howe Island stick insect.
exercise: What does extinct mean? | Not one of that kind is left alive anywhere | Very rare but still living somewhere out in the wild | Living only in zoos | Asleep for the winter
exercise: What kind of bird was the dodo? | A big flightless pigeon | A small flying parrot from Africa | A kind of short-legged ostrich | A penguin from the Arctic
exercise: On which island did the dodo live? | Mauritius | New Zealand | South Georgia | Madagascar
exercise: What really killed off the dodo? | Sailors and the rats, pigs and monkeys they brought | Dodos were too stupid to find enough food to eat each day | A volcano covered the island | Dodos forgot how to lay eggs
exercise: Why did the dodo not fly away or fear people? | It had never met predators on its island | It was too lazy to flap its short, stubby wings | It liked people very much | It was blind
exercise: What was the name of the last passenger pigeon? | Martha | Dolly | Lucy | Matilda
exercise: Why did the passenger pigeon die out? | Mass hunting and the cutting of forests | A single, very cold winter in America | It flew away to another continent for good | Cats ate them all in one year
exercise: What was the thylacine? | A striped, meat-eating marsupial shaped like a wolf | A real tiger that lived wild in the forests of Australia | A kind of large striped cat | A wild dog from Africa
exercise: When did the last known thylacine die? | In 1936, in Hobart Zoo | In 1690, on the island of Mauritius | In 2006, in a zoo in China | It is still alive in many zoos
exercise: What was Steller's sea cow? | A giant, gentle cousin of the manatee | A farm cow that swam out to islands to graze | A kind of walrus with tusks | A huge seal from Antarctica
exercise: How quickly was Steller's sea cow hunted out after Europeans found it? | In 27 years | In 270 years | In just 2 days | In 2,700 years
exercise: Which bird was the first to be called a penguin? | The great auk | The emperor penguin | The Atlantic puffin | The dodo of Mauritius
exercise: What was special about the quagga's stripes? | It had stripes only on its front half | It had spots, not stripes | It had stripes only on its four legs and tail | It had red and white stripes
exercise: What is blamed for the loss of the golden toad? | A deadly fungus disease and a changing climate | Hunters who wanted its golden skin to make jewellery | A flood in the desert | Toad-eating birds from Europe
exercise: Where did the baiji live? | Only in China's Yangtze River | In every ocean | In the Amazon River | In Lake Victoria and the Nile in Africa
exercise: About how much of all species that ever lived are now extinct? | More than 99 percent | About half | About 10 percent of them | About 1 percent
exercise: What is a Lazarus species? | An animal thought extinct that turns up alive again | An animal brought back to life from its old DNA | An animal that lives in churches | An animal that never ever dies
exercise: Which is a Lazarus species? | The coelacanth | The dodo of Mauritius | The passenger pigeon | The striped quagga
test: In a cartoon, the dodo is a dim, clumsy bird that trips over its own feet. What is the real story? | It was not stupid; it had never met predators and could not cope with the animals people brought | It really was too stupid to live, so it walked up to hunters and could not learn to run away | It died out because it could not walk | It is still living on Mauritius
test: «the dodo was too dumb to survive, natural selection innit» What is right? | No, it was well fitted to an island without predators until people brought new ones | Yes, it was the dumbest bird ever, so nature got rid of it long before people came | Yes, it forgot to eat | No, it was killed by a meteorite
test: Count them: a picture shows 3 dodos and 2 great auks. How many legs and how many wings are there? | 10 legs and 10 wings | 10 legs and 0 wings | 20 legs and 10 wings | 5 legs and 5 wings
test: «my cousin saw a Tasmanian tiger in the bush last year, so they're not extinct» What is the best answer? | Many sightings are reported, but none have been proven | Yes, so it is certainly alive and well in the deep bush | Yes, there are thousands of them | No, Tasmanian tigers were never real
test: «there were billions of passenger pigeons, you can't kill billions» What is right? | People did: mass hunting and forest cutting killed them all by 1914 | Right, they are still flying today in huge flocks over the forests of Canada | They all flew to Europe | They turned into ordinary pigeons
test: What would most likely have happened to the dodo if sailors had never brought rats, pigs and monkeys? | It might have lasted much longer on its island | It would have learnt to fly at once | It would have swum to Africa | It would have turned into a penguin
test: «species die out all the time anyway so who cares» What is the best answer? | Extinction is natural, but today it is much faster than normal because of people | Right, extinction never matters at all, because new animals always take their place | No species ever died out before people | Only big animals can go extinct
test: Count them: a zebra and a quagga stand in a museum. How many tails, ears and legs do they have in all? | 2 tails, 4 ears and 8 legs | 2 tails, 2 ears and 8 legs | 1 tail, 4 ears and 8 legs | 2 tails, 4 ears and 4 legs
test: «penguins live at the South Pole, so the first penguin must've been there» What is right? | The first bird called a penguin was the great auk of the North Atlantic | Penguins started at the North Pole | The dodo was the first penguin | Penguins were first seen in Australia
test: The news says a frog thought extinct for 50 years was found in a forest. What is this called? | A Lazarus species | A cloned species | A missing link | A new continent
test: «they made new quaggas from DNA, so it's back from extinction» What is closer to the truth? | A project bred plains zebras to look like quaggas; the original quagga is still gone | Yes, real quaggas were made from old DNA in a laboratory and now live again in the wild | The quagga never went extinct | Quaggas were made from horse DNA
exam: Identify the principal cause of the extinction of the dodo. | The arrival of people together with rats, pigs and monkeys introduced to its island | An innate deficiency of intelligence, which prevented it from obtaining sufficient food | A volcanic eruption upon Mauritius | A failure to adapt to cold climates
exam: State the name and place of death of the last known passenger pigeon. | Martha, in Cincinnati Zoo, in 1914 | Lucy, in Ethiopia, in 1974 | Dolly, in Edinburgh, in 2003 | Martha, in Hobart Zoo, in 1936
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rate of extinction at present. | Species are presently vanishing considerably faster than the natural rate, owing to human activity | Extinction has ceased entirely in the present age | The present rate is identical to that of all former ages | Extinction occurs exclusively among large mammals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a Lazarus species? | A species formerly believed extinct which is subsequently found to survive | A species recreated from preserved DNA | A species that has never been recorded | A species confined to religious buildings
